"Cross-references people or roles with defined skills to expose capability coverage, development needs and reliance on individuals.\n\nUse when: Plan cross-training and operational coverage. Compare required capabilities with assessed current proficiency.\n\nAvoid when: Unvalidated ratings would be used as a definitive measure of personal performance.\n\nVisual grammar: Rows and columns distinguish people or roles from skills. Cells encode proficiency levels with explicit behavioral anchors. Separate markers identify required levels, current evidence and development priorities.\n\nBuild: Choose skills that matter for the team's actual responsibilities. Define observable proficiency criteria and the assessment method. Record current coverage and identify development or backup needs.\n\nCheck: Proficiency and confidence are not silently treated as identical. Ratings have an assessment date and comparable criteria. Required coverage accounts for availability as well as skill presence.\n\nPitfalls: Using vague labels such as expert without observable anchors. Assuming one qualified person guarantees resilient capability coverage.\n\nExample: A visitor center maps language support, cash handling and first aid to identify shift coverage gaps and useful cross-training.\n\nAlternatives: Team charter canvas, RACI matrix."
